# 14. Map Requirements to Source Data

Next, connect each business requirement to the source data needed to calculate it.

| Requirement | KPI / Attribute | Likely Source |
|---|---|---|
| Sales | Revenue | Order Details |
| Sales | Orders | Orders |
| Sales | Units Sold | Order Details |
| Product | Product Revenue | Order Details + Products |
| Product | Category | Products + Categories |
| Sales Agent | Agent Revenue | Orders + Employees |
| Inventory | Units in Stock | Products |
| Inventory | Reorder Level | Products |
| Customer | Customer Revenue | Orders + Order Details |
| Customer | Purchase Frequency | Orders |
| Customer | Geography | Customers |
| Supplier | Supplier | Products + Suppliers |

Now we know which operational tables are required for the warehouse.

---

# 15. Define the Grain

Before creating a fact table, we must clearly define:

> **What does one row represent?**

For `fact_sales`, a good grain is:

> **One row per product line item on an order.**

Suppose Order `10248` contains:

```text
Order 10248
Customer: VINET

Product A       12 units
Product B       10 units
Product C        5 units
```

The fact table would contain:

```text
Order     Product     Quantity
--------------------------------
10248     Product A      12
10248     Product B      10
10248     Product C       5
```

This grain allows Northwind to analyze both:

**Order-level performance**

and

**Product-level performance.**

---

# 16. Define Facts and Dimensions

Once the grain is established, we can identify measurable facts.

For sales:

```text
Quantity
Unit Price
Gross Sales
Discount Amount
Net Sales
Freight
```

For example:

```text
Gross Sales
= Quantity × Unit Price

Discount Amount
= Quantity × Unit Price × Discount

Net Sales
= Gross Sales - Discount Amount
```

Dimensions provide the descriptive context:

```text
Customer
Product
Date
Employee
Supplier
Geography
Shipper
Category
```

---

