# 18. Inventory Model

Inventory is a separate business process.

A possible grain would be:

> **One row per product per inventory snapshot date.**

For example:

```text
fact_inventory_snapshot
--------------------------------
date_key
product_key
supplier_key

units_in_stock
units_on_order
reorder_level
inventory_value
```

Connected to:

```text
                 dim_date
                    │
                    ▼
dim_product ─ fact_inventory ─ dim_supplier
                    │
                    ▼
               dim_category
```

---

# 19. Create the Kimball Bus Matrix

Now we can document which dimensions are shared across business processes.

| Business Process | Date | Customer | Product | Category | Employee | Supplier | Geography | Shipper |
|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| Sales | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Inventory | ✓ |  | ✓ | ✓ |  | ✓ |  |  |

The matrix helps identify **conformed dimensions**.

For example, `dim_product` should mean the same thing whether someone is analyzing sales or inventory.

```text
                 dim_product
                      │
             ┌────────┴────────┐
             ▼                 ▼
        fact_sales      fact_inventory
```

This creates consistency across the warehouse.

---

# 20. Establish Naming Conventions

Standards should be established before development grows.

For example:

| Object | Standard | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Fact | `fact_<process>` | `fact_sales` |
| Dimension | `dim_<entity>` | `dim_customer` |
| Surrogate Key | `<entity>_key` | `customer_key` |
| Source ID | `<entity>_id` | `customer_id` |
| Date | `<event>_date` | `order_date` |
| Timestamp | `<event>_at` | `created_at` |
| Boolean | `is_<condition>` | `is_discontinued` |
| Amount | `<metric>_amount` | `discount_amount` |

This creates a predictable and maintainable warehouse.

